ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]2[ab]"},"output":""}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def decodeString(self, s: str) -> str:\n        stack = []          # (string_before_bracket, repeat_count)\n        current = []        # characters of the string being built\n        count = 0\n        for ch in s:\n            if ch.isdigit():\n                count = count * 10 + int(ch)\n            elif ch == \"[\":\n                stack.append((\"\".join(current), count))\n                current, count = [], 0\n            elif ch == \"]\":\n                prev, k = stack.pop()\n                current = [prev + \"\".join(current) * k]\n            else:\n                current.append(ch)\n        return \"\".join(current)\n","approach":"**Key insight: every `[` opens a new context that must be finished before the enclosing one can continue.** Saving the enclosing context on a stack and restoring it at `]` decodes arbitrarily nested input in one pass.\n\n**Algorithm.** Keep `current` (the string being built) and `count` (digits seen since the last non-digit). On a digit, extend `count`. On `[`, push `(current, count)` and reset both. On `]`, pop `(prev, k)` and set `current = prev + current * k`. Letters are appended to `current`. The final `current` is the answer.\n\n**Why it works.** The stack mirrors the bracket nesting: each level holds the text decoded so far at that depth and the multiplier waiting for the bracket to close. Because the input is valid, every `]` pops the context pushed by its matching `[`.\n\n**Common mistakes.** Taking only one digit for the count (`10[x]`). Forgetting to reset `count` after `[`. Building strings with repeated `+=` inside deep nesting, which can go quadratic; a list of parts or a single join per level keeps it linear in the output size. Writing a recursive solution that returns the string but loses track of the index where the recursion stopped.\n\n**Interview follow-ups.** (1) Return only the `k`-th character of the decoded string without expanding it (work with lengths). (2) Decode a stream or a very long string where the output does not fit in memory. (3) Allow `k` to be omitted (meaning 1) or allow nested counts like `2[3]`.","complexity":{"time":"O(n + m) where m is the output length","space":"O(n + m)"},"similar":["basic-calculator","number-of-atoms","decoded-string-at-index"],"followups":["Return the k-th decoded character without expanding.","Recursive descent version."],"number":36,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def decodeString(self, s: str) -> str:\n        pass\n"}